{#
  Migration 0109 — THE RLS SCOPE TOKEN HAS A SOURCE.

  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 ("other triggers, same shape": `fn_site_role` — fill the RLS scope token)
  · §4.1 law 10 · §11.3 · §16 MI01 · verticals/mainline/db/migrations/0020a_site.sql.

  Row-level security policy expressions CANNOT CONTAIN SUBQUERIES on this platform. Tenancy scope
  is therefore a denormalised role-name token compared directly against the session role:

      USING (site_role = CURRENT_USER)

  Which means: IF THE WRITER SUPPLIES THE TOKEN, THE WRITER CHOOSES WHICH TENANT CAN SEE THE ROW.
  Row-level security degrades into an application-cooperative control, which is worthless against
  exactly the adversary it exists to constrain. Behind every such token there must be a row
  somebody provisioned, and reading it from that row — refusing when it is absent — is the whole
  of this function.

  TYPE `NAME`, NOT `STRING`, AND THAT IS NOT PEDANTRY. `CURRENT_USER` has type `NAME`. A
  comparison across an implicit cast is a comparison a future release is entitled to change the
  semantics of, and the failure mode of a tenancy predicate that silently stops matching is ZERO
  ROWS, FOREVER, WITH NO ERROR — indistinguishable from a tenancy control that works, right up
  until someone notices a site has never seen its own data.

  WHY THIS FILE SHIPS A FUNCTION AND NO TRIGGER.
  The tables carrying `site_role` are the gated subjects, and the kernel's acyclicity ruling is
  that THE ONLY TRIGGER ON A GATED SUBJECT TABLE IS THE MERGE GATE, whose WHEN clause restricts it
  to the merge transition. A BEFORE INSERT trigger on a subject table would not in fact create a
  cycle — the obligation triggers reach those tables by UPDATE, never by INSERT, so an insert-only
  trigger adds no depth — but the ruling is stated without that exception and this worker does not
  own the subject DDL, so this file does not weld it and does not pretend to.

  THE CONSEQUENCE IS NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE DISCOVERED: until something welds this function
  (or an equivalent) to every relation carrying `site_role`, that column is INSERTER-SUPPLIED, and
  the row-level security scope is forgeable by any role holding INSERT. That is a live gap, it is
  recorded in the rendered SQL where a reviewer reads it, and it belongs to whoever owns the
  subject tables and the acyclicity ruling — not to a comment in a template.

  GENERIC BY CONSTRUCTION. The body names `site_id` and `site_role` and nothing else, so it welds
  unchanged to any relation carrying that pair. That is the reason it is a function in the
  substrate band rather than an inline fragment in one table's migration.

  PLATFORM (measured on CockroachDB CCL v26.2.5, 2026-08-09): `(NEW).col` for every read; a `NAME`
  local variable is accepted in `DECLARE` and assigns cleanly to a `NAME` column.
#}
